# The Myth of the First Stars

Long ago, when the sky was empty and dark, the Moon wept in loneliness. She wandered the endless black void with no companions, no light to reflect, no beauty to mirror.

One night, a curious child climbed the highest mountain, reaching toward the heavens. "Why do you cry?" the child called up to the Moon.

"I am alone," the Moon replied, her voice echoing across the darkness. "There is nothing but emptiness here."

The child thought deeply, then began to gather fireflies from the meadows below. One by one, the child released them skyward, where they flew higher and higher, their lights growing brighter and brighter. But when they reached the Moon, they began to fade.

Seeing the child's kindness, the Moon blessed each firefly with eternal light. They transformed into stars, scattering across the heavens in brilliant patterns. The Moon smiled for the first time, her face glowing with reflected starlight.

The child returned to earth, but every clear night, the stars remember that generous heart. They twinkle especially bright when children look up in wonder, reminding us all that even the smallest lights can illuminate the darkest skies.
